*** About this fork ***

This is an altered version of Echo, maintained at
https://github.com/drojaazu/echo. It is not the original software, which is
Sik's: https://github.com/sikthehedgehog/echo

It is based on upstream commit e326c87 (Echo 1.68a). Its changes are all in
the Z80 program; the 68000 side and the API are the same.

   * Echo comes in two builds, one tuned for NTSC machines and one for PAL
     machines, and both FM and PSG notes are tuned to the clocks of the
     machine the build is for:

        built/prog-z80.bin      NTSC (src-z80/build.z80)
        built/prog-z80-pal.bin  PAL  (src-z80/build-pal.z80)

     c/echoblob.h holds both; define ECHO_PAL when compiling c/echo.c to
     use the PAL one. Upstream has one build: its FM table is tuned for NTSC,
     and its PSG table sits between the NTSC and PAL clocks, with each
     octave above the lowest made by halving the one below and rounding
     down. So upstream's PSG notes play about 10 cents sharp of FM notes on
     either machine type, and up to 119 cents sharp at the top (A#8 and B-8
     share a value).

     In the NTSC build, the FM table is Echo's own and the PSG table is
     round(3579545 / 32 / f) with A-4 = 440 Hz. In the PAL build, the FM
     table is Echo's scaled by the ratio of the clocks, and the PSG table is
     round(3546895 / 32 / f). doc/esf.txt lists both.

     NOTE: the NTSC build was checked on an actual Japanese NTSC Mega Drive:
     every PSG note plays at the frequency its period predicts. The PAL values
     are calculated from the PAL clocks and have only been tested in emulators,
     not on a PAL machine.

     Music tuned by ear against upstream's table will come out a little
     flatter in PSG, more in the top octaves, and periodic noise played from
     PSG3 notes moves with it. Songs that set raw frequencies with the
     3-byte $30-$3A events are not retuned by either build.

   * A PSG instrument's semitone shift can't take a note past either end of
     the frequency table any more: the shifted note is clamped to C-3..B-8.
     Upstream reads past the table there, and the bytes it finds can write
     another channel's volume instead of a frequency.

=============================================================================

WARNING: if you used Echo_SetVolumeEx in Echo 1.4, beware that the ABI
changed to require 16 bytes instead of 13. Currently the extra bytes are
ignored, but consider adapting your code (just set them to zeroes).

